  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/28c4751694caf50f08f73d7ccf3525877e6679901d49854951810f550a65ede2.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope lines on the same horizontal line are all the same. This means that the slope does not vary with $x.$ In other words, ${y}^{\u2032}$ is a function of $y$ only.\n\n- Second, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{y}$ is a function of $y$ only, but\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{y}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only.\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{2}{y-1}$ is a function of $y$ only, and \n\n$$\n|{y}^{\u2032}|=|\\frac{2}{y-1}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
